# Error reference

Every non-2xx response from the certificate service is an RFC 9457
`application/problem+json` document, and every operation failure carries the same
vocabulary in `operation.error.code`. The `code` member is the contract: **branch on
`code` alone**, never on the HTTP status, never on the `title` or `detail` text. A
casing or spelling mismatch is a client bug that no compiler catches and that no fake
service notices, because the fake was built from the same wrong assumption.

Codes are **immutable once published** -- never renamed, never re-spelled, never given
a different meaning, never reused after retirement. A new condition gets a new code,
and a withdrawn code is retired rather than deleted, so a name that ever meant
something never comes back meaning something else.

A code this page does not list is **not** a bug in your client. It is a newer service
talking to an older provider: render the response's own `next_action`, `title`,
`detail` and `request_id`, and treat the condition as non-retryable.

Every code has a stable type URI of `https://mantisec.dev/acme/errors/<code>`.

