reverted by the contract drift check. Operator remediation procedure for a code is a
runbook, and is deliberately not on this page.

## How to read a row

| Column | What it tells you |
|---|---|
| `Code` | The machine-readable contract. This, and only this, is what a client branches on. |
| `HTTP` | The status the code is rendered with. `--` for a code that only ever appears inside an operation record. |
| `Surface` | `http` for an HTTP response, `op` for an operation record, `both` for either. An `op` row names the field the code lands in. |
| `Retryable` | Authoritative for the retry classifier. When the body does not parse, fall back to the status: retry 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, transport errors, and 409 **when it carries `Retry-After`**. |
| `Actor` | Who can actually act. Anything other than `caller` means the fix is not in your configuration. |
| `Attribute` | The dotted wire path of the offending field, where the condition has one. |
| `Meaning` | The condition, in one line. |
| `Next action` | The imperative next step. Where the actor is not `caller`, it names the party to ask. |

### Who can act

| `actor` | Who that is |
|---|---|
| `caller` | The team writing the HCL. |
| `namespace_owner` | The owner of the namespace. |
| `platform_admin` | A platform administrator of the certificate service. |
| `vault_owner` | The owner of the destination Key Vault. |
| `dns_owner` | The owner of the DNS zone carrying the challenge name. |
| `ca` | The certificate authority. Nothing to do but wait. |
| `client_bug` | A defect in the API client. The Terraform provider maintainers own the fix. |

